-## Can the new legislature cut the property tax on Myrtle Beach rentals?
-Not with Republican votes alone. South Carolina's constitution taxes the home you live in on 4 percent of its value, and every other home on 6 percent.
-The legislature can change those numbers only with two-thirds of each chamber's members. That is 83 of 124 in the House and 31 of 46 in the Senate.
-Seats after the 2026 election. R is Republicans and D is Democrats. The brass line is the two-thirds vote it takes to change the tax ratio on rentals and second homes. House seats are the unofficial count.
-Republicans hold 34 Senate seats, more than 31. In the House they won 79, so even if every Republican votes yes, a change needs at least 4 Democratic votes. A new property tax exemption needs the same two-thirds.
-The other path is a constitutional amendment. It needs two-thirds of each chamber, then a majority of voters in November 2028, then a second vote by the next legislature.
-Our page on Wilson's rental tax cut works through what a lower ratio would save on a rental here.
