-### Where a buyer tax credit was tried before
-The federal credit of 2008 to 2010 paid up to $8,000. A study in the Journal of Finance found it raised home sales by about 490,000, near 10 percent. It raised prices about 1 percent by the study's measure. Much of the extra demand came from buyers who would have bought several years later. The authors judged that its benefit in steadying the market likely exceeded its direct boost to buying.
-Here it did not stop a fall. The Myrtle Beach-area price index dropped about 10 percent in 2009 and kept falling until 2012.
+Taxed like a home you live in $2,040
 
-### What it could mean for a $50,000 credit
-If a $50,000 credit passed, the same pattern points to more sales and higher prices in the first years. It is about six times the old credit, and no study we found measured a credit that size. How much prices would rise, then or later, is unknown. Our page on the first-time buyer tax credit shows what the last one did.
